Enable the nkilib shard-on-I context-encoding MoE kernel at LNC=1 (NUM_SHARDS=1).

Target: nkilib rev 1ee625782cb1bf91b40bccab741a82c726445080
Apply from the nkilib repo root:  git apply -p1 nkilib-lnc1-moe-cte.patch
Applied automatically by contrib/qwen3.6-35b-a3b/deploy/compile_prefill_trn2.sh.

Why: prefill's MoE calls blockwise_mm_baseline_shard_intermediate_hybrid, which
hard-asserted NUM_SHARDS == 2 with the message "shard-on-I with dynamic control
flow only work on TRN2". That message conflates TRN2 *hardware* with a 2-shard
LNC=2 *logical core* -- we are on TRN2 hardware at either LNC setting, so it is a
shard-count guard, not a hardware one. Blocked TP=8/LNC=1 prefill at compile time
with [NCC_INKI016].

Four changes, all no-ops at NUM_SHARDS == 2 (LNC=2 codegen is unaffected):

  1. blockwise_mm_baseline_shard_intermediate: drop the 1-shard core_barrier over
     a single-core group; only barrier when there is actually a peer core.
  2. blockwise_mm_baseline_shard_intermediate_hybrid (the prefill entry point):
     accept NUM_SHARDS in (1, 2), and make the three (0, 1) core_barriers -- two
     on `output`, one on the shared dynamic-loop counter -- conditional on having
     a peer core. nl.dynamic_range itself is not shard-dependent.
  3. compute_down_proj_shard_on_intermediate (shared by all three entry points):
     at NUM_SHARDS == 1 there is no peer to sendrecv the down-proj partials with,
     and the peer source index b + NUM_B_TILES_SHARDED * (1 - shard_id) runs off
     the end of block_new_lst. Alias block_new_lnc_recv_sbuf_lst to block_new_lst
     (identical length/shape/dtype at 1 shard) and skip the exchange+reduce
     entirely. Also saves NUM_B_TILES * TILE_SIZE * H of SBUF versus allocating a
     second tile set and copying into it -- SBUF per core is halved at LNC=1, so
     this is not just cosmetic. Safe because nothing reads block_new_lst after the
     reduction loop.
  4. blockwise_mm_shard_intermediate_dropping: same 1-shard barrier relaxation as
     (1). Not on the prefill path; kept consistent across the three entry points.

If the aliasing in (3) ever proves problematic, the fallback is to keep the
separate allocation and nisa.activation(op=nl.copy) the local tile into it.

